  <h2>Sound natural: let stress reveal the emotional target</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>In natural U.S. speech, <span class="ex">wish I</span> can link closely, and auxiliaries often reduce. Keep the time-carrying verb recoverable. Stress normally falls on the contrast the speaker wants changed, not automatically on <span class="ex">wish</span>.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Written form</th><th>Common connected speech</th><th>Meaning cue</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>I wish I were <b>THERE</b>.</td><td><span class="ex">wish I</span> may link like /ˈwɪʃaɪ/</td><td>Stress the missing place or state.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>I wish I’d <b>CHECKED</b>.</td><td><span class="ex">I’d</span> = I had before a participle</td><td><span class="ex">checked</span> locates the unreal completed past.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>I wish it’d <b>STOP</b>.</td><td><span class="ex">it’d</span> = it would before a base verb</td><td><span class="ex">stop</span> is the desired change.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>If only I could’ve <b>STAYED</b>.</td><td><span class="ex">could have</span> may sound like /ˈkʊdəv/</td><td>Standard writing retains <span class="ex">could have</span>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>I wish I hadn’t <b>SENT</b> it.</td><td><span class="ex">hadn't</span> may reduce, but <span class="ex">sent</span> stays clear</td><td>Stress can contrast the regretted action.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">If only</span> often forms one opening thought group. Its pitch may express longing, frustration, or regret, but there is no single compulsory “if only intonation.” Context and contrastive stress carry the attitude.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read wish-I linking, contracted had and would, weak could-have, negative Past Perfect, and contrastive-stress listening check" data-success-message="Correct. You used the following verb, real time, and stress to recover five meanings from connected U.S. speech.">
    <h3>Listen before reading the options closely</h3>
    <p class="note-line">Tutor: read each line naturally twice. Student: choose the meaning, then explain which later verb or stressed word resolved the reduced form.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Wish listening choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a different present location">a different present location</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a regretted completed check">a regretted completed check</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a desired process change">a desired process change</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="an unavailable past opportunity">an unavailable past opportunity</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="regret about sending, not receiving">regret about sending, not receiving</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a certain future plan">a certain future plan</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Tutor reads: <span class="ex">“I wish I were THERE.”</span> The line expresses <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a different present location" data-hint="Were marks present counterfactual distance, and THERE carries the contrast." data-fix="The speaker is not there now and prefers a different present location.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Tutor reads: <span class="ex">“I wish I'd CHECKED the date.”</span> <span class="ex">’d</span> is recovered as had because the line expresses <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a regretted completed check" data-hint="Checked is a participle after contracted had, and the checking opportunity is over." data-fix="I wish I had checked the date expresses a regretted completed check.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Tutor reads: <span class="ex">“I wish it'd STOP sending those alerts.”</span> <span class="ex">’d</span> is would because the line expresses <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a desired process change" data-hint="Stop is a base verb after contracted would, and the alerts keep occurring." data-fix="The speaker wants the alert process to change now.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Tutor reads: <span class="ex">“If only I could've STAYED.”</span> The reduced auxiliary expresses <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="an unavailable past opportunity" data-hint="Could have plus stayed points to past ability or opportunity that was unavailable." data-fix="If only I could have stayed expresses an unavailable past opportunity.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Tutor reads: <span class="ex">“I wish I hadn't SENT it so soon.”</span> The stress identifies <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="regret about sending, not receiving" data-hint="The stressed lexical participle names the action the speaker wants to undo." data-fix="The regret concerns sending it too soon.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
