  <h2>Read a debrief without turning hindsight into blame</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional Pine Street open-day file</h3>
    <p>Registration opened on September 2 for a September 15 open day. The main room's posted capacity was 60, and 74 registrations were accepted before the team closed the form. The second room had already been reserved for an electrical inspection on September 15, so it could not be used for the event.</p>
    <p>Before launch, the team tested the form on two desktop browsers and two current phones. It did not test three older phone models. On September 2, 43 people attempted registration on those three models, and 18 of those attempts were incomplete. The available record does not establish that phone compatibility caused every incomplete attempt.</p>
    <p>On September 15, the reminder system sent one message at 8:00 a.m. and a duplicate at 8:05. The vendor confirmed the duplicate process at 9:10 and predicted a fix by noon. At 11:30, the fix was still being tested, so completion remained an open future possibility.</p>
    <p>A careful debrief can say, <span class="ex">We wish we had capped registration at 60</span> and <span class="ex">If only we had tested the older phones</span>. It can also say, <span class="ex">We wish the reminder system would stop sending duplicates</span> and <span class="ex">We hope the vendor finishes by noon</span>. The later outcome supports better safeguards, but it does not prove that one person could have predicted every problem or caused every incomplete attempt.</p>
  </div>

