  <h2>Practice reflection without exposing a private regret</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>Use a safe distance</h3>
    <p>You never need to discuss a real relationship, medical decision, financial loss, immigration matter, disciplinary event, academic failure, family conflict, legal issue, or painful regret. Use the fictional Pine Street file, a movie character, or a low-stakes invented situation. Emotional precision does not require personal disclosure.</p>
    <p>When a past outcome is known now, avoid turning hindsight into false certainty. <span class="ex">I wish we had tested the phones</span> expresses regret and suggests a safeguard. It does not by itself prove that the test would have predicted every incomplete attempt or that one person was responsible.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Reality map:</b> Use Pine Street or a tutor-created fictional project. State four real facts, locate each one as present or completed past, and build the preferred alternative only after your listener can recover the actual timeline.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Attitude switch:</b> Keep one fictional situation constant. Express it as an unreal state, unavailable ability, completed regret, or desired change, adding the contextual fact needed to make each interpretation defensible.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Wish or hope:</b> Create one deadline that is still open and one that has passed. Use <span class="ex">hope</span> for the open result and Past Perfect after <span class="ex">wish</span> for the completed alternative.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Spoken contrast:</b> Say one sentence with contracted <span class="ex">had</span> before a participle and one with contracted <span class="ex">would</span> before a base verb. Ask your listener to recover each auxiliary from the following verb.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Responsible close:</b> Turn one fictional regret into a practical safeguard. State what the later result supports and what it does not prove about cause, prediction, or individual responsibility.</div>

  <h2>Final production and check</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Final production: lead a 90-second reality-and-response debrief</h3>
    <p>Use Pine Street or another fictional project. Include one present state you wish were different, one unavailable present ability with <span class="ex">could</span>, two past regrets with Past Perfect, one desired behavior or process change with <span class="ex">would</span>, and one genuinely open outcome with <span class="ex">hope</span>. State the real fact before each preferred alternative.</p>
    <p>Add one negative, one question about another person's wish, one contracted <span class="ex">’d</span> form whose meaning the next verb makes clear, one evidence limit, and one practical next step. Your partner should challenge one sentence by asking, <span class="ex">Is that a state, an unavailable ability, a completed regret, or a changeable process?</span> Reformulate immediately if the form and attitude do not match.</p>
    <p><b>Listener check:</b> Can the listener recover real time, preferred reality, speaker attitude, and the evidence limit without guessing?</p>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You can now map reality to form, distinguish wish from hope, control would and could, hear contracted auxiliaries, and keep regret separate from blame.">
    <h3>Seven final decisions</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="I wish the main room were larger." data-hint="The room's size is a stable current state." data-fix="I wish the main room were larger."><p class="quiz-question">1. Which sentence gives a careful unreal present state?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="I wish the main room were larger.">I wish the main room were larger.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="I wish the main room would have been larger now.">I wish the main room would have been larger now.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="I hope the main room had been larger now.">I hope the main room had been larger now.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="If only we had tested the older phones before launch." data-hint="The unperformed test belongs before a completed launch." data-fix="If only we had tested the older phones before launch."><p class="quiz-question">2. Which sentence expresses the completed past regret?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="If only we had tested the older phones before launch.">If only we had tested the older phones before launch.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="If only we would test the older phones before the past launch.">If only we would test the older phones before the past launch.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="If only we test the older phones yesterday.">If only we test the older phones yesterday.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="I wish the reminder system would stop sending duplicate messages." data-hint="The duplicate process keeps occurring and can be changed." data-fix="I wish the reminder system would stop sending duplicate messages."><p class="quiz-question">3. Which sentence asks for a repeated process to change?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="I wish the reminder system would stop sending duplicate messages.">I wish the reminder system would stop sending duplicate messages.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="I wish the reminder system stopped yesterday before it existed.">I wish the reminder system stopped yesterday before it existed.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="I hope the reminder system had stopped now.">I hope the reminder system had stopped now.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="We hope the vendor finishes by noon because the fix is still possible." data-hint="The future deadline remains open at 11:30." data-fix="We hope the vendor finishes by noon."><p class="quiz-question">4. Which line fits an open future result?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="We hope the vendor finishes by noon because the fix is still possible.">We hope the vendor finishes by noon because the fix is still possible.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="We wish the vendor had finished by noon tomorrow because tomorrow is over.">We wish the vendor had finished by noon tomorrow because tomorrow is over.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="We wish to the vendor finishes by noon.">We wish to the vendor finishes by noon.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="What do you wish you had known before registration opened?" data-hint="Invert do and the main subject, but keep statement order in the embedded clause." data-fix="What do you wish you had known before registration opened?"><p class="quiz-question">5. Which question has correct embedded order?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="What do you wish you had known before registration opened?">What do you wish you had known before registration opened?</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="What do you wish had you known before registration opened?">What do you wish had you known before registration opened?</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="What you wish did you had known before registration opened?">What you wish did you had known before registration opened?</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="In I wish I'd checked, ’d means had because checked is a participle." data-hint="Use the form after the contraction to recover the hidden auxiliary." data-fix="I'd checked expands to I had checked."><p class="quiz-question">6. Which explanation correctly recovers contracted <span class="ex">’d</span>?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="In I wish I'd checked, ’d means had because checked is a participle.">In I wish I'd checked, ’d means had because checked is a participle.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="In I wish I'd checked, ’d means would because would takes a participle directly.">In I wish I'd checked, ’d means would because would takes a participle directly.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The contraction has no recoverable grammatical meaning.">The contraction has no recoverable grammatical meaning.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The later result supports a testing safeguard, but it does not prove one cause or one person's foresight." data-hint="Keep the useful retrospective lesson and both evidence limits." data-fix="The result supports a safeguard without proving one complete cause or one person's foresight."><p class="quiz-question">7. Which conclusion separates regret from unsupported blame?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The later result supports a testing safeguard, but it does not prove one cause or one person's foresight.">The later result supports a testing safeguard, but it does not prove one cause or one person's foresight.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Saying I wish we had tested proves that one employee knowingly caused all 18 incomplete attempts.">Saying I wish we had tested proves that one employee knowingly caused all 18 incomplete attempts.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Because causation is incomplete, no future safeguard can be justified.">Because causation is incomplete, no future safeguard can be justified.</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Reflect: can you name the reality before the wish?</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Explain the full choice</h3>
    <p>Complete these lines aloud: <span class="ex">The real fact is...</span> <span class="ex">Its time is...</span> <span class="ex">The preferred alternative is...</span> <span class="ex">My attitude is state / ability / regret / desired change...</span> <span class="ex">That is why I used...</span> <span class="ex">If only adds...</span> <span class="ex">Hope is better here because...</span> <span class="ex">The evidence supports...</span> <span class="ex">The evidence does not prove...</span> <span class="ex">A realistic next step is...</span></p>
    <p>Then change one time fact. Move a current problem into the completed past, or reopen a past deadline so the result becomes possible again. Reformulate the sentence and explain why the old grammar no longer fits.</p>
    <p><a href="#next-day-retrieval">Return tomorrow for the five-item retrieval check</a> before rereading the lesson.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Next use:</b> Before the next lesson, retrieve one unreal present, one unavailable ability, one past regret, one desired change, and one open hope from five new fictional facts. Explain the time and attitude before checking the form.</div>

